<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	What the app says of itself that Xcode's build settings have no setting for. The rest
	of the built app's Info.plist (its name, version, identifier and so on) comes from the
	target's build settings in Pro7ChordEditor.xcodeproj, and Xcode puts the two together.

	NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription: ProPresenter keeps its library in the Documents
	folder, and macOS asks the person, the first time, whether this app may read there.
	These are the words it shows them under its question (spec/10-platforms.md).

	CFBundleIconFile: the app's icon, which is the file ChordEditor.icns beside this one.
	It is the maintainer's own picture, given as that file on 2026-10-13, and is in the
	app as it was given: Xcode copies it into the app's Resources with everything else
	in this folder, and this names it. (Not an asset catalogue: that would have Xcode
	make the icon's sizes again from pictures taken out of the file, to no gain.)
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
	<string>ChordEditor</string>
	<key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>To open the songs in your ProPresenter library.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
